.TH "SKARN-SETUP" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.17.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-setup \- Wire the skarn guard hook into detected AI coding agents
(merge\-based, reversible), then verify it with the guard self\-test
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBsetup\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Wire the skarn guard hook into detected AI coding agents (merge\-based,
reversible), then verify it with the guard self\-test.
.PP
Guided onboarding: detect installed AI coding agents (Claude Code, Cursor,
Codex CLI), merge the skarn guard hook into each one's native config in audit
mode, and prove the wiring with the guard self\-test. Existing foreign hooks
are never touched; every modified file is backed up first
(*.skarn\-bak.<timestamp>) and rewritten atomically; a target file that does
not parse as JSON is refused, never overwritten. On a terminal it runs an
interactive wizard with default\-yes prompts; \-\-yes accepts everything,
\-\-print writes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Target agents, repeatable or comma\-separated. Default: all detected (an agent
is detected when its config directory exists or its CLI is on PATH); naming an
agent explicitly also targets it when undetected. One of: claude, cursor,
codex, all.
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
Guard mode written into the hook commands. Audit records would\-be verdicts to
the guard log without blocking; start there, review the log, then flip to
enforce. Enforce needs a license to actually block; an unlicensed guard runs
audit\-only regardless. One of: audit, enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Add \-\-strict to the generated guard commands (also gates Read operations;
fail\-closed semantics on Codex).
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
Guard log path wired into the hook commands as the SKARN_GUARD_LOG prefix;
pass none to disable logging. The Codex commandWindows form carries no prefix
(set the variable in the environment on Windows). The default is
~/.cache/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl.
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fIpath\fR
The guard executable invoked by the hook commands. The default resolves this
binary's absolute path (preferring the stable Homebrew <prefix>/bin symlink
over the versioned Cellar path) because GUI\-launched hosts like Cursor do not
inherit brew's PATH; pass skarn to opt into PATH\-relative. The default is
this binary's absolute path.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR
Write user\-level configs (~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json,
~/.codex/hooks.json) or project\-level ones (./.claude, ./.cursor, ./.codex
relative to the working directory). One of: user, project. The default is
user.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\fR
Print the final per\-agent config to stdout and write nothing; the
paste\-it\-yourself and fleet\-template path.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With \-\-print: json emits one {agent, path, content} envelope per agent for
machine consumption. One of: text, json. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Non\-interactive: accept every prompt's default. Required when running without
a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Only touch agents whose config already carries skarn entries; the
audit\-to\-enforce flip is skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
Surgically remove skarn\-owned hook entries, dropping any matcher groups and
event arrays they leave empty. Foreign hooks are untouched, the file itself is
never deleted, and backups from earlier runs are kept.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print a machine\-readable result summary (per agent: path, action, backup,
self\-test result) instead of the text report.
